ulatory Hormone Excess / Paraneoplastic Endocrine", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.3594251674147285, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The subacute timeline supports beta-cell destruction (type 1 diabetes) over chronic insulin resistance mechanisms, though paraneoplastic syndromes would typica…"], "evidence_against": ["Classic paraneoplastic syndromes typically include neurological features, but some hormone-excess states may present with non-neurological multisystem symptoms"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Insulin Deficiency (Beta-Cell Loss)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.5473758999581484, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The subacute timeline supports beta-cell destruction (type 1 diabetes) over chronic insulin resistance mechanisms, though paraneoplastic syndromes would typica…"], "evidence_against": ["Severe hyperglycemia without metabolic acidosis/ketosis is atypical for beta-cell destruction, suggesting alternative mechanisms like glucagonoma or rare insul…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.25, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Insulin Resistance", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.03541263547762913, "posterior": 0.0005054003019791103, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Obesity is a well-established risk factor for insulin resistance, and the abdominal pain may represent a secondary metabolic complication, providing modest sup…"], "evidence_against": ["The rash pattern is more typical of paraneoplastic syndromes like glucagonoma than isolated metabolic disorders, but the absence of neurological deficits remai…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.35, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["type 2 diabetes mellitus"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Insulin Excess (Hypoglycemia Distractor)", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.093, "danger": 0.1, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Glucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor) with Paraneoplastic Dermatologic Syndrome", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2567322624390918, "posterior": 0.2567322624390918, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "ACTH-Secreting Tumor (Ectopic Cushing's Syndrome)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.10269290497563674, "posterior": 0.10269290497563674, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Autoimmune Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.3421099374738428, "posterior": 0.3421099374738428, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Idiopathic Beta-Cell Destruction (Type 1b Diabetes)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.20526596248430562, "posterior": 0.20526596248430562, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B2.1", "B2.2", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of metabolic acidosis/ketosis (excluded by the vignette) contradict the diagnosis of Beta Cell Destruction (e.g., type 1 diabetes) as a cause of severe hyperglycemia?", "summary": "The lack of acidosis/ketosis does not definitively exclude beta-cell destruction, as hyperglycemia can occur without decompensation in some contexts."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset over days and the presence of a painful erythematous rash (not typically associated with insulin resistance) argue against this branch?", "summary": "The subacute progression and painful rash are atypical for isolated insulin resistance, which typically manifests with more chronic metabolic patterns."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of a painful erythematous rash with papules/plaques on face, torso, and extremities support the paraneoplastic dermatologic syndrome mechanism in this case?", "summary": "The rash pattern is more typical of paraneoplastic syndromes like glucagonoma than isolated metabolic disorders, but the absence of neurological deficits remains a key concern for B1"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute 1-week progression of symptoms with multisystem involvement (rash, GI, constitutional) support Beta Cell Destruction as the underlying cause of hyperglycemia?", "summary": "The subacute timeline supports beta-cell destruction (type 1 diabetes) over chronic insulin resistance mechanisms, though paraneoplastic syndromes would typically have more variable temporal patterns"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of localized neurological deficits (a hallmark of paraneoplastic syndromes) argue against the diagnosis of Counter-Regulatory Hormone Excess / Paraneoplastic Endocrine?", "summary": "Classic paraneoplastic syndromes typically include neurological features, but some hormone-excess states may present with non-neurological multisystem symptoms"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the severe hyperglycemia >500 mg/dL with no prior diabetes history and absence of metabolic acidosis/ketosis contradict Beta Cell Destruction as the primary mechanism?", "summary": "Severe hyperglycemia without metabolic acidosis/ketosis is atypical for beta-cell destruction, suggesting alternative mechanisms like glucagonoma or rare insulin deficiency variants"}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.563], ["B1", 0.267], ["B4", 0.135]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.547], ["B1", 0.359], ["B2.1", 0.342]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability markers present", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch B2 (Beta-cell destruction) has posterior=0.547 <0.75, and B1 (paraneoplastic) remains a moderate-probability alternative (posterior=0.359). Dangerous alternatives like B1.1 (glucagonoma, danger=0.7) still exist unresolved."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.72, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3421099374738428, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Alpha cell tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Beta cell destruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Beta cell tumor"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Hypercortisolism"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Insulin resistance"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Erythematous rash', 'Hyperglycemia', 'Insulin insensitivity', 'Beta-cell dysfunction'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.563}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2.1", "label": "Autoimmune Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus", "posterior": 0.342, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.1", "B2.2", "B1.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=50%, source=both]\n\nInsulin Resistance vs Glucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor) with Paraneoplastic Dermatologic Syndrome:\n  Favours first:  areas of darkened skin, autosomal dominant inheritance, blurred vision, fatigue, frequent infections\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nInsulin Resistance vs Autoimmune Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus:\n  Favours first:  areas of darkened skin, frequent infections, increased hunger, increased waist to hip ratio, insulin resistance\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, bed-wetting in children, confusion, darkened skin patches, dry mouth\n\nGlucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor) with Paraneoplastic Dermatologic Syndrome vs Autoimmune Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, autosomal dominant inheritance, bed-wetting in children, blurred vision, confusion\n\nRelated to Insulin Resistance: diabetes mellitus (disease), diabetes mellitus, noninsulin-dependent, diabetic ketoacidosis\n\nRelated to Glucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor) with Paraneoplastic Dermatologic Syndrome: humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy, oncogenic osteomalacia, paraneoplastic neurologic syndrome\n\nRelated to Autoimmune Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus: autoimmune disease of endocrine system, autoimmune polyendocrine, diabetes mellitus (disease)\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.60):]\n  Source: A review of cutaneous manifestations within glucagonoma syndrome: necrolytic migratory erythema. > Treatment / Management\n  Curative treatment of glucagonomas and its accompanying paraneoplastic syndrome is centered around early surgical excision of the tumor, which, as previously mentioned, is located in the tail of the p\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.69):]\n  Source: A review of cutaneous manifestations within glucagonoma syndrome: necrolytic migratory erythema. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Necrolytic migratory erythema (NME) is a characteristic skin rash most often associated with glucagonoma, an alpha-cell tumor of the pancreatic islets. It is usually seen as a part of the glucagonoma \n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.57):]\n  Source: A review of cutaneous manifestations within glucagonoma syndrome: necrolytic migratory erythema. > Treatment / Management\n  Curative treatment of glucagonomas and its accompanying paraneoplastic syndrome is centered around early surgical excision of the tumor, which, as previously mentioned, is located in the tail of the p\n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.62):]\n  Source: A review of cutaneous manifestations within glucagonoma syndrome: necrolytic migratory erythema. > Introduction\n  Necrolytic migratory erythema (NME) is a characteristic skin rash most often associated with glucagonoma, an alpha-cell tumor of the pancreatic islets.[1] It is usually seen as a part of the glucagono\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  a painful rash → necrolytic migratory erythema → Glucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor) with Paraneoplastic Dermatologic Syndrome\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate if the rash has the classic appearance of necrolytic migratory erythema (crusted, erythematous, blistering lesions with a 'goose-egg' appearance) to confirm paraneoplastic dermatologic syndrome associated with glucagonoma\n  abdominal pain and diarrhea → glucagon excess syndrome → Glucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor) with Paraneoplastic Dermatologic Syndrome\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Assess for additional features of glucagon excess syndrome (weight loss, anemia, tongue fissures) that would support the paraneoplastic connection\n  obesity and smoking history → metabolic syndrome → Insulin Resistance\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Quantify metabolic syndrome components (waist circumference, dyslipidemia, hypertension) to strengthen the insulin resistance hypothesis\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
